1️⃣ Face Authentication — this ones genuinely strong, lean into it 💪
How liveliness detection actually works

Not a simple "blink detection" — it's smile detection using 3 combined signals on facial landmarks from face-api.js:

MAR (Mouth Aspect Ratio) — mouth height ÷ mouth width. A smile changes this ratio measurably.
Corner elevation — smiling lifts the corners of your mouth relative to the center — measured as a normalized distance.
Lip curvature — the upper lip curves during a genuine smile, measured via a handful of central landmark points.

These 3 scores get combined into a weighted confidence score: MAR×0.4 + elevation×0.4 + curvature×0.2, and only counts as a real smile if confidence crosses 0.6 for 2 consecutive frames (prevents a random single-frame glitch from passing).

Why this matters: this is real anti-spoofing logic — a static photo held up to the camera can't "smile" on command, so this blocks the most basic photo-based spoofing attack. It won't stop a video replay attack, but it's a legitimate first layer.

How Aadhaar face matching works

EnhancedFaceApiService extracts a face descriptor (a 128-number vector representing facial features) from both the Aadhaar photo and the live camera photo, then computes Euclidean distance between the two vectors. Smaller distance = more similar faces. There's a threshold (0.42 for live comparisons, stricter 0.38 for document-to-document) — if distance ≤ threshold, it's a match.

Smart detail: thresholds are DYNAMICALLY adjusted based on image quality — blurry/small/low-quality faces get a stricter effective threshold, since low quality reduces reliability of the match. That's a genuinely thoughtful engineering decision.

