🎭 Face Authentication

Walk me through how face authentication works.
"Two images get processed — the user's Aadhaar photo and a live camera capture. face-api.js extracts a 128-dimensional face descriptor from each using a face landmark and recognition model. We compute the Euclidean distance between the two descriptors — smaller distance means more similar faces — and if that distance falls under a threshold, it's considered a match."

What is a face descriptor?
"It's a numerical vector — essentially a compressed representation of facial features — output by a face recognition neural network. Two photos of the same person produce descriptors that are close together in that vector space; different people produce descriptors that are far apart."

Why Euclidean distance?
"It's the standard metric face-api.js is built around and validated for — the underlying model was trained so that Euclidean distance between its output descriptors correlates with facial similarity. It's simple, fast to compute, and works well for this use case."

Why different thresholds for document vs live photos?
"Aadhaar document photos are typically lower resolution, sometimes slightly different lighting or angle than a live selfie, so a document-to-live comparison naturally has more baseline variance than live-to-live. We use a stricter threshold (0.38) for document comparisons and a slightly more lenient one (0.42) for general live comparisons, and additionally adjust dynamically based on measured image quality."

How does liveliness detection work? Why smile, not blink?
"It's smile detection using three combined signals on facial landmarks — mouth aspect ratio, mouth corner elevation, and lip curvature — combined into a weighted confidence score, requiring 2 consecutive frames above threshold to confirm. I'd guess smile was chosen over blink because smiling produces a larger, more measurable change in landmark geometry than a blink, which can be subtle and easy to miss depending on frame rate and eye shape variation."

What is MAR and how is it calculated?
"Mouth Aspect Ratio — the vertical distance between the top and bottom lip center points, divided by the horizontal distance between the mouth corners. A smile widens the mouth and typically increases this ratio measurably compared to a neutral expression."

Why 2 consecutive frames, not 1?
"A single frame could register a false positive from a brief facial twitch, motion blur, or landmark detection noise. Requiring 2 consecutive frames above the confidence threshold filters out those one-off glitches while still keeping the check fast — checking every 200ms means confirming across roughly 400ms of sustained smiling."

Would holding up a photo of a smiling person fool this?
⚠️ Honest answer, and a good one to give proactively: "Yes, potentially — this liveliness check verifies a smile is present in the current frame, but it doesn't inherently distinguish a live face from a high-quality printed or displayed photo of a smiling person. It blocks the simplest spoof — a neutral static photo — but wouldn't catch a more deliberate photo-based attack. A stronger implementation would add depth-sensing, blink randomization, or challenge-response prompts." This is a great, mature answer — shows you understand the LIMITS of your own security feature, which interviewers respect a lot.

Poor lighting/bad angle?
"The detection would likely fail to find a face at all, or the quality score would drop, which pushes the effective match threshold stricter — so a bad-quality live capture is actually less likely to authenticate successfully, which is the safer failure mode."

